y can promote it right through the  planning process  um at pre-application meetings in  discussions um between builders and  developers  right through the process including the  building rigs team this is the kind of  key point that we want to make here we  can't make this mandatory that we know  the law won't allow that at the moment  there's a frustration really that the  levels of building  standards are not what they really need  to be  and they're moving in the right  direction but this guide if we promoted  it if we pushed it to people  in the planning process  uh would be something that would help  them it would guide them  uh it would it would lead them in the  right direction and could make some real  impacts  in reducing um uh reducing the amount of  carbon emitted by our homes the ones  we've built already and the ones we're  going to build in the future so i  commend this uh this motion to the to  the council thank you chad  thank you councilman uh could i have a  second to please  counselor field  do you wish to speak now  wait  okay  right we will now move to debate  catholic  i'd like to make a point of information  if i'm a chair  thank you  this is this is actually very timely  because  we held our suffolk public sector  leaders  planning meeting on friday  at which  as chair of spsl  i approved this document for use across  the whole of suffolk councils  so  clearly  you did you didn't know about that  and  to be fair most on this side didn't know  either but  the um there was a a bit of a  discrepancy in the process that was  followed  but  the whole of the suffolk public sector  leaders group on friday authorized me to  sign off this document and distribute it  so with your permission i'd like to read  you the forward that i've i've placed in  that document  suffolk's local authorities have a  shared and collective ambition to work  towards the aspiration of a carbon  neutral suffolk by 2030  and a key theme of our climate emergency  plan is the